WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.240 --> 00:00:01.240
- [Instructor] When we humans look

00:00:01.240 --> 00:00:02.540
at the universe around us,

00:00:02.540 --> 00:00:04.640
we're blown away by the complexity

00:00:04.640 --> 00:00:06.920
and the diversity of
all the things we see,

00:00:06.920 --> 00:00:10.660
from the scale of things
in the cosmos like stars.

00:00:10.660 --> 00:00:12.080
This is a picture of our sun,

00:00:12.080 --> 00:00:14.480
all the way down to a more human scale,

00:00:14.480 --> 00:00:16.630
and all the way down to
the microscopic scale.

00:00:16.630 --> 00:00:19.360
This is a visualization of a coronavirus.

00:00:19.360 --> 00:00:22.090
It seems like there's an infinite number

00:00:22.090 --> 00:00:23.570
of things out there.

00:00:23.570 --> 00:00:26.130
But an interesting
question has always been,

00:00:26.130 --> 00:00:28.420
are there some basic building blocks,

00:00:28.420 --> 00:00:31.160
things, a smaller set,
a finite set of things

00:00:31.160 --> 00:00:35.210
that we can put together to
make up all of this complexity?

00:00:35.210 --> 00:00:36.480
Ancients thought about,

00:00:36.480 --> 00:00:38.710
maybe it's fire, water, wind,

00:00:38.710 --> 00:00:41.620
but now we know that there
are things called elements,

00:00:41.620 --> 00:00:43.220
and we have classified them

00:00:43.220 --> 00:00:45.340
on the periodic table of elements.

00:00:45.340 --> 00:00:48.750
And we know today of about 118 elements,

00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:50.490
but we might discover more.

00:00:50.490 --> 00:00:52.870
But what's mind blowing is all matter

00:00:52.870 --> 00:00:55.460
that we know of in the universe is made up

00:00:55.460 --> 00:00:58.320
of combinations of these elements.

00:00:58.320 --> 00:01:00.470
Now, what's even more amazing is most

00:01:00.470 --> 00:01:03.190
of what we experience in our
everyday lives is made up

00:01:03.190 --> 00:01:06.050
of an even smaller
subset of these elements.

00:01:06.050 --> 00:01:09.860
For example, almost 99% of the mass

00:01:09.860 --> 00:01:14.160
on Earth is made up of
these eight elements.

00:01:14.160 --> 00:01:15.670
And if we look at ourselves,

00:01:15.670 --> 00:01:18.520
if you were to stare at your
hand or look in the mirror,

00:01:18.520 --> 00:01:20.180
life, as we know it,

00:01:20.180 --> 00:01:24.530
is made up of mainly these six elements:

00:01:24.530 --> 00:01:29.060
hydrogen, carbon, nitrogen,
oxygen, phosphorus, and sulfur.

00:01:29.060 --> 00:01:32.680
Now, the earth and life is
made up of other elements,

00:01:32.680 --> 00:01:35.250
but it is amazing how much complexity

00:01:35.250 --> 00:01:37.960
and how much can be made with combinations

00:01:37.960 --> 00:01:41.900
of the things that we see in
the periodic table of elements.

00:01:41.900 --> 00:01:43.650
Now, the next question is, all right,

00:01:43.650 --> 00:01:46.640
we can make things out of these elements,

00:01:46.640 --> 00:01:48.960
but what if we were to
keep cutting an element

00:01:48.960 --> 00:01:51.240
or what if we were to keep
cutting a substance down?

00:01:51.240 --> 00:01:55.130
What is the smallest
unit that still maintains

00:01:55.130 --> 00:01:58.000
the properties of an element?

00:01:58.000 --> 00:02:01.060
And that unit, you have
probably heard the word,

00:02:01.060 --> 00:02:04.980
is known as an atom,

00:02:04.980 --> 00:02:08.200
and atoms can be further broken down.

00:02:08.200 --> 00:02:10.300
In fact, an atom has a nucleus

00:02:10.300 --> 00:02:11.690
and inside that nucleus,

00:02:11.690 --> 00:02:16.340
it has subatomic
particles known as protons

00:02:16.340 --> 00:02:19.420
and neutrons in that nucleus.

00:02:19.420 --> 00:02:22.390
And then you could imagine buzzing around,

00:02:22.390 --> 00:02:24.940
and we're talking about
very, very small scale,

00:02:24.940 --> 00:02:26.190
and it's hard to pinpoint.

00:02:26.190 --> 00:02:28.530
It's more of a probability
where you might find these,

00:02:28.530 --> 00:02:32.490
but buzzing around that
nucleus, you have electrons,

00:02:32.490 --> 00:02:35.560
and the protons have a positive charge,

00:02:35.560 --> 00:02:37.540
the neutrons have a neutral charge,

00:02:37.540 --> 00:02:40.750
and the electrons have a negative charge.

00:02:40.750 --> 00:02:43.350
But everything we're
talking about is made up

00:02:43.350 --> 00:02:46.230
of combinations of these
three fundamental particles,

00:02:46.230 --> 00:02:47.920
and what's especially cool is

00:02:47.920 --> 00:02:50.780
a given atom is mostly free space.

00:02:50.780 --> 00:02:52.680
You have the dense nucleus

00:02:52.680 --> 00:02:55.270
but then these atoms
are just buzzing around

00:02:55.270 --> 00:02:57.890
when mostly free space.

00:02:57.890 --> 00:03:00.960
And what actually gives
an atom the properties

00:03:00.960 --> 00:03:03.300
of a specific element is the number

00:03:03.300 --> 00:03:05.170
of protons we're dealing with.

00:03:05.170 --> 00:03:06.330
So for example,

00:03:06.330 --> 00:03:10.090
what makes an atom a hydrogen
is it has one proton.

00:03:10.090 --> 00:03:15.090
What makes an atom a chlorine
is that it has 17 protons.

00:03:15.240 --> 00:03:17.210
The number of electrons can change.

00:03:17.210 --> 00:03:18.610
That will change its charge

00:03:18.610 --> 00:03:21.900
and that might change how it
interacts with other atoms,

00:03:21.900 --> 00:03:26.270
but it still will be classified
as a, for example, carbon,

00:03:26.270 --> 00:03:28.640
regardless of how many electrons it has.

00:03:28.640 --> 00:03:30.120
And similarly, the number

00:03:30.120 --> 00:03:31.920
of neutrons don't affect its charge,

00:03:31.920 --> 00:03:34.270
but it will affect the mass
that we're dealing with.

00:03:34.270 --> 00:03:37.191
But even if you change
the number of neutrons

00:03:37.191 --> 00:03:39.160
and you have the same number of protons,

00:03:39.160 --> 00:03:40.550
you're still dealing,

00:03:40.550 --> 00:03:42.220
if you say you have six protons,

00:03:42.220 --> 00:03:44.450
no matter how many neutrons
or electrons you have,

00:03:44.450 --> 00:03:46.280
you're still dealing with carbon.

00:03:46.280 --> 00:03:48.300
And it's really interesting to appreciate

00:03:48.300 --> 00:03:52.910
just how small atoms are.

00:03:52.910 --> 00:03:55.160
If you think about it just as a number,

00:03:55.160 --> 00:03:57.230
the width of, let's say, a carbon atom,

00:03:57.230 --> 00:03:58.340
and there are smaller atoms,

00:03:58.340 --> 00:04:00.710
like a hydrogen atom or a helium atom,

00:04:00.710 --> 00:04:04.570
but the width of a carbon
atom is roughly one times 10

00:04:04.570 --> 00:04:06.870
to the negative 10th meters.

00:04:06.870 --> 00:04:08.270
Another way to think about it

00:04:08.270 --> 00:04:11.300
and it depends on the context
you're seeing the carbon,

00:04:11.300 --> 00:04:13.710
but roughly speaking, if
you took 10-billion carbons

00:04:13.710 --> 00:04:16.560
and put them side by side, it
would be about a meter wide.

00:04:16.560 --> 00:04:19.360
Or if you wanna blow
your mind even further,

00:04:19.360 --> 00:04:21.210
if you had about 10-million carbons

00:04:21.210 --> 00:04:24.290
and put them side by side, that
would be about a millimeter.

00:04:24.290 --> 00:04:26.280
So try to approximate a millimeter.

00:04:26.280 --> 00:04:28.170
A millimeter, depending on
the resolution of your screen,

00:04:28.170 --> 00:04:29.700
might be something like that.

00:04:29.700 --> 00:04:32.630
There would be 10-million carbons,

00:04:32.630 --> 00:04:35.040
depending on how big you're
looking at this video,

00:04:35.040 --> 00:04:37.530
just in that space over there.

00:04:37.530 --> 00:04:39.970
If that doesn't blow your mind enough,

00:04:39.970 --> 00:04:42.670
another way to think
about it is it's estimated

00:04:42.670 --> 00:04:46.380
that there are more
atoms in a grain of sand

00:04:46.380 --> 00:04:48.450
than there are grains of sand

00:04:48.450 --> 00:04:51.510
in all of the beaches on Earth.

00:04:51.510 --> 00:04:53.810
And if that doesn't blow your mind,

00:04:53.810 --> 00:04:56.570
roughly speaking, if you took an apple

00:04:56.570 --> 00:05:01.300
and if you made the apple
the size of the earth,

00:05:01.300 --> 00:05:06.300
then an atom in that apple
would be the size of an apple.

00:05:06.630 --> 00:05:09.070
You can hear that again and process that.

00:05:09.070 --> 00:05:10.700
If you took, or another
way to think about it,

00:05:10.700 --> 00:05:13.160
an atom of an apple is to the apple

00:05:13.160 --> 00:05:17.400
as an apple is to all of Earth.

00:05:17.400 --> 00:05:20.860
So atoms are tiny, tiny, tiny.

00:05:20.860 --> 00:05:22.480
So I'm gonna leave you there.

00:05:22.480 --> 00:05:24.860
That's enough mind blowing for one video.

00:05:24.860 --> 00:05:27.640
And then in future videos,
we'll discuss more in depth

00:05:27.640 --> 00:05:31.790
about atoms and the subatomic
particles that make them up,

00:05:31.790 --> 00:05:32.670
and we'll talk more

00:05:32.670 --> 00:05:35.773
about classifying the
different types of matter.

